ID: B3-Ch05b-Refreshed
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch5_passed,1) if (v_b3_ch5_moustache == 2)
achievement("Challenge accepted",v_ac_b3_ch5_passed)
 When our four hours run out, we head back to the barracks to wake Kate and Leila. While the two of them lace up their boots for the final watch, Hadrik rolls off his cot, grunting that he wants an empty corridor to drill his shifting forms before sunrise. Once the three of them head out into the dark, Flower, Melindra, and I collapse onto the rough blankets. Sleep takes me hard and does not let go until boots scrape against the stone floor four hours later, when the room is already stirring and packs are being buckled in the grey gloom.

 Melindra and I haul our gear together, hail Hadrik on the transceiver, and meet him in the passage outside so we can make for the central staging hall as a group.

 "Holding up, Hadrik?" Daren asks, falling into step and resting a heavy gauntleted palm on the dwarf's shoulder. "If yesterday is still sitting heavy, you can talk to us. You do not have to carry it alone."

 "Save the bedside manner, Daren, I am in one piece," Hadrik says, his grin flashing through his thick beard. "Yesterday was a bad turn, that is all. A few hours of sleep and some corridor drills, and I am itching for the rematch. When do we go up?"

 "The moment we collect Kelrim," Daren says. "He will be in quarantine already. I will pull him out as soon as we reach the waiting room."

 "Are Arraka and Illuna awake in there," I ask, "or do we have to knock on the metal?"

#if(v_b3_ch5_moustache == 2) {
 "I am awake, idiot," Arraka's voice snarls from the gold amulet at Flower's neck. "And I already washed off that ridiculous ink moustache with a splash of water, in case you were admiring your handiwork. Truly inspiring tactical discipline, Barry-boy. Selkram will tremble when you draw eyebrows on him during our final showdown."

 "Is that a dare?" I ask.

 "Take it however you like," she says.

 "Good to know. The moment I unlock magical stats, I am inventing five distinct facial-hair curses just for the God of Time. What about Illuna? Is she awake, or still brooding?"

 "She is awake, but she will not talk to you," Flower says, adjusting the heavy gold chain. "I have been calling her since I opened my eyes, and she just keeps grumbling that she is busy mastering a difficult spell and will not speak until it is finished."

 "A difficult spell?" I glance at the amulet. "If it helps against royal guards, she can take all morning. Leave her to it."

 We follow the curving tunnels into the main waiting room. The shouting and frantic movement from nine hours ago have thinned, replaced by the dull, hollow murmur of hundreds of displaced surface dwellers huddled along the walls. Blankets and straw pallets spill into every alcove; the rebels clearly ran out of spare quarters long before dawn.

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_moustache != 2) {
 "I am awake, idiot," Arraka's voice snarls from the gold amulet at Flower's neck.

 "What about Illuna?" I ask.

 "She is awake, but she will not talk to you," Flower says, adjusting the heavy gold chain. "I have been calling her since I opened my eyes, and she just keeps grumbling that she is busy mastering a difficult spell and will not speak until it is finished."

 "A difficult spell?" I glance at the amulet. "If it helps against royal guards, she can take all morning. Leave her to it."

 We follow the curving tunnels into the main waiting room. The shouting and frantic movement from nine hours ago have thinned, replaced by the dull, hollow murmur of hundreds of displaced surface dwellers huddled along the walls. Blankets and straw pallets spill into every alcove; the rebels clearly ran out of spare quarters long before dawn.

}
 Albert is waiting near the archway. The moment our boots cross the threshold, he pushes off the stone wall and marches straight toward us, dark circles bruised under his eyes and his jaw set tight. Hauling refugees through the underground tunnels all night has stripped away whatever patience he had left.

 "The king of the underground has requested a meeting with you," Albert says, the formal courtesy grating through clamped teeth. "He is waiting for you in the throne room. Follow me."

choice(""Sure thing. Let's go."", B3-Ch05b-Tension, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Tension, v_b3_ch5_follow = 0)
choice(""Psst, Albert! You forgot to put on your fake smile when you asked us to come with you."", B3-Ch05b-Tension, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Tension, v_b3_ch5_follow = 1)
choice(""Your king can wait. First, we need to find Kelrim."", B3-Ch05b-Tension, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Tension, v_b3_ch5_follow = 2)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Tension
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch5_follow == 0) {
 "Sure thing. Let's go."

 Albert gives a stiff nod and turns down the passage, leading the way toward the throne room while the rest of us fall into step behind him.

 The corridors wind deeper into the bedrock until the tunnel opens into the high-vaulted chamber where the king waits. We pull out the empty wooden chairs facing the dais and take our seats. Albert strides past our row, taking up a position beside Peter behind the stone throne. The two royal guards who usually flank the seat are nowhere in sight. The only people in the room are the king, Peter, Albert, and our group.

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_follow == 1) {
 "Psst, Albert! You forgot to put on your fake smile when you asked us to come with you."

 "I did not forget," Albert says, venom dripping from every syllable. "I see no reason to smile at this moment, and especially not to you. Now, are you going to follow me or not?"

 "The king would not call for us at dawn unless something went wrong," Daren says, stepping in before Albert's temper boils over. "Let us hear what he has to say. Lead the way."

 Albert gives a stiff nod and turns down the passage, leading the way toward the throne room while the rest of us fall into step behind him.

 The corridors wind deeper into the bedrock until the tunnel opens into the high-vaulted chamber where the king waits. We pull out the empty wooden chairs facing the dais and take our seats. Albert strides past our row, taking up a position beside Peter behind the stone throne. The two royal guards who usually flank the seat are nowhere in sight. The only people in the room are the king, Peter, Albert, and our group.

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_follow == 2) {
 "Your king can wait. First, we need to find Kelrim."

 "I don't think I've made myself clear enough," Albert says, stepping directly into my path, his hand twitching near his belt. "I wasn't giving you a choice. You WILL follow me to the throne room."

 "Or what?" I ask, holding my ground.

 Albert's jaw clenches, his eyes narrowing into cold slits as he takes another step forward.

 "Easy, both of you," Daren says, planting himself between us with a raised hand. "We are on the same side here, or close enough to it. If the king's business cannot wait, Kelrim can wait a few minutes. I take it he is in the quarantine hall?"

 "The goblin is in quarantine," Albert says through gritted teeth. "You can consult him when we finish. Move."

 "Understood," Daren says. "Lead the way."

 Albert gives a stiff nod and turns down the passage, leading the way toward the throne room while the rest of us fall into step behind him.

 The corridors wind deeper into the bedrock until the tunnel opens into the high-vaulted chamber where the king waits. We pull out the empty wooden chairs facing the dais and take our seats. Albert strides past our row, taking up a position beside Peter behind the stone throne. The two royal guards who usually flank the seat are nowhere in sight. The only people in the room are the king, Peter, Albert, and our group.

}
 Exhaustion hangs heavy over all three of them. Dark hollows shadow the king's eyes, and his posture is rigid. Underneath the fatigue, the air in the chamber feels thick and cold, a quiet pressure that was not here during our previous audiences.

 "Good morning, friends," the king says. "I trust you found some rest. Forgive the abrupt summons, but events on the surface leave us no time for courtesy. First, some context."

 He draws a small metal cube from his coat, identical to the listening device he demonstrated during our first meeting, and sets it on his palm.

 "Your friend Eiden went out of his way to herd several key figures into one of the few palace chambers where we maintain listening devices," the king says. "He clearly intended for you to hear the recording, and I intend to honor his design. This took place yesterday, shortly after most of you were scattered into the failsafe tunnels below the city. The voices should be familiar to you. Listen."

 He depresses a switch on the cube. Static hisses for a fraction of a second, followed by voices echoing from the tiny speaker. The first cadence is unmistakable.

 "Is this the room that you wanted to bring us to?..." the ambassador of Ollendor asks.

 "Yes," Eiden says, his light tone cutting cleanly through the recording. "In here we should be safe from any spies that might be trying to listen to our conversation."

 "I assure you, Creator, that there are no spies in this palace!" the king of Ollendor declares. "There would be no way for them to get past our guards!"

 "I do not doubt your guards' competence, great king," Eiden replies. "But still, it is better to be safe than sorry."

 "What is it that you wanted to speak to us about, ambassador?" the captain of the royal guards asks.

 "I... do not know where to begin..." the ambassador stammers. "It is difficult to put into words. My king, it is with a heavy heart that I bring news from Thilias. Your brother... our beloved earl... has been murdered."

 Silence falls over the recording. No one speaks. Five seconds stretch into ten before the ambassador risks another word.

 "My king?..." the ambassador whispers, the pitch of his voice rising in panic.

 "Ambassador, I want you to think very carefully about your next words," the king of Ollendor says, his tone dead and terrifyingly level. "What you say next decides whether you leave this room alive. First, confirm what you just said. Are you absolutely certain my brother is dead?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Bypass, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Bypass)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Bypass
TEXT:

 "Y-yes, your highness," the ambassador stammers, his voice quavering through the speaker. "I am certain of it. We brought his body back as proof. Our mages did everything possible to preserve the flesh with stasis spells, but it has been more than a week since the deed. The sight of him will be... difficult."

 "How did it happen?" the king asks, his voice remaining terrifyingly flat.

 "He was... ambushed in the middle of a public street by tournament contestants," the ambassador says. "The earl fought valiantly, but the mages used underhanded tactics to--"

 "Tournament contestants?" the king interrupts. "How is that possible? Why did the ward not cast them back to their home continents the instant they drew blood?"

 "Yes, I am curious as well!" Eiden's voice floats in, bright with theatrical wonder. "Tell us, ambassador, how were those treacherous mages able to circumvent such an airtight, unbendable protection spell?"

 Across the recording, I can picture the stillwater's closed-eyed smirk as clearly as if he were standing in the room, waiting for the diplomat to squirm.

 A long, heavy silence hangs over the device before the captain speaks. "Are you going to answer, ambassador?"

 "We... do not know how they bypassed the spell," the ambassador whispers. "However, we have reason to believe they can harm any local on this continent without restriction."

 "And these contestants... were they executed for treason?" the king asks, his calm tone fraying at the edges.

 "...No, your highness," the ambassador says. "King Golmyck refused execution outright. We reached a compromise and forced them into the arena without magic, but they survived the trial. Under the continental treaty, the gnomes released them the same day--"

 "You let them walk?!" The king of Ollendor's roar tears through the speaker, distorting against the metal casing so violently that Daren flinches. "You miserable worm! You stand there and tell me my brother's murderers are wandering free across Varathia? I should throw you in chains and feed your entrails to the hounds! Is this why I sent you to Thilias? To grovel before that idiot gnome as if you were his servant? Why did you not threaten him with war on the spot? You let him wipe his boots on you like a palace cur?!"

 "My king, I threatened war, but he would not listen!" the ambassador cries in panic. "And Golmyck was not the only one shielding them! The real reason they were sent to the arena was because..."

 The words cut off sharp. The ambassador chokes on his own breath, realizing the cost of naming Eiden in front of the crown.

 "Because..." the ambassador quavers, his voice trembling violently.

 I know that calculation. Caught between the king's executioner and a smiling stillwater who tears armies apart for entertainment, there is no good answer.

 "If I may, your highness," the guard captain cuts in, filling the void before the king can strike. "The intruders we clashed with yesterday were tournament contestants as well, and they attacked me without hesitation. A fully armored, dark-skinned mage with an X-shaped scar across his forehead, an ice-wielding banshee, a child hurling fire, and a giant compressed into a dwarf. Does that match the earl's killers, ambassador?"

 "That is them!" the ambassador shouts, gasping with relief. "The very same ones! I am certain of it!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Manhunt, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Manhunt)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Manhunt
TEXT:

 "So my brother's killers were inside my walls this whole time..." the king of Ollendor says, his anger hardening into cold venom. "Captain, you reported that your men nearly trapped them before they vanished. Are they still within the city?"

 "Without question, your highness," the captain replies. "Biliborrathrathsarran assured us that his barrier prevents any conventional teleportation beyond our outer walls. The only way in or out is through the gates. Wherever these criminals are burrowed, it is inside Ollendor."

 "Then the course is simple," the king says. "Captain, turn this city inside out. Wake every garrison, search every alley, cellar, and attic until there is no shadow left unturned. If citizens bar their doors, treat it as treason and put them to the sword where they stand. No one enters or leaves Ollendor until every one of my brother's murderers is hanging from the gallows. We lock down the entire city, and you, ambassador, will oversee the hunt alongside the captain."

 "Me?..." the ambassador squeaks.

 "You," the king says. "The outcome of this purge rests on your head. Fail me again, and you will not have the luxury of an arena. Your desire to keep breathing should sharpen your resolve."

 "Yes, your majesty," the ambassador stammers. "Of course..."

 "Good," the king says. "Start the preparations. I want a complete deployment plan in under an hour. And captain... dispatch a runner to summon Bilibo... Bilibo... the lessathi immediately. Tell him the crown demands his presence. Surely he has a device that can track these rats through the streets."

 "At once, my king," the captain says.

 "I will meet you both in the throne room once the deployments are set," the king says. "Do not keep me waiting."

 Heavy bootsteps recede across the stone floor as the king and captain exit the chamber. Once the echo fades, Eiden's light, mocking voice returns to the speaker.

 "Aren't you glad the captain cut you off, ambassador?" Eiden murmurs. "Thanks to his interruption, our little secret stays between us, and you live to see another sunrise. Make the most of your day, ambassador. Life is fragile. You never know when it might snap."

 "Yes... Creator," the ambassador mutters, every word dripping with forced deference. "I thank you for the reminder."

 Footsteps follow, and the recording falls dead. The king of the underground clicks the switch, pockets the cube, and looks up at us from the throne.

 "Well?" he asks into the silence. "What do you think?"

choice(""I think the quality of the recording was very good."", B3-Ch05b-Quality, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Quality)
choice(""I... guess the cat's out of the bag?"", B3-Ch05b-Bag, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Bag)
choice(""I swear we were going to tell you about the fact that we killed the king's brother!"", B3-Ch05b-Brother, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Brother)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Quality
TEXT:

 "I think the quality of the recording was very good."

 "As do I," the king of the underground says evenly. "We spent a considerable sum on those devices, and they perform admirably. Now, is there anything of substance you wish to add?"

 "Listen," Daren says, leaning forward with his hands open. "I know how this looks, but we can fix it."

 "People are dying up there right now, healer," the king says, his voice dropping into a grave, flat register. "The royal guards are tearing through the districts, kicking down doors and executing anyone who hesitates. We evacuated the rebel families from the surface, but there is neither the space nor the air beneath the bedrock to shelter the entire population. Innocent civilians are being slaughtered in their homes because the palace is hunting you. Those people would be alive if you had never entered this city. Their blood is on your hands."

 "I understand, and I am sorry," Daren says, his jaw tightening. "We did not expect the ambassador to arrive so quickly, and we failed to anticipate the backlash. But our goal has not changed. It aligns with yours. If we organize a combined assault against the king of Ollendor, we can--"

 "You misunderstand your standing, healer," the king cuts in. "The only reason the royal guard has not cornered you is that we gave you shelter. I am grateful for your work in the quarantine ward—that is why I let you sleep through the night while the city burned above us. But I cannot trade the lives of the surface population for the slim hope of curing a handful of terminal patients."

 "The hope is not slim!" Daren insists. "Kelrim and I identified the root of the illness. With the right reagents from the surface, we can cure those people today."

 "And once you have cured them, what then?" the king asks. "Will you surrender to the royal guard? Or do you have another plan to halt the bloodshed on the streets?"

 "We finish what we started," Daren says. "We strike down the king of Ollendor and end the terror."

 "Strike down the king?" Albert sneers from behind the throne. "The lot of you could not handle two royal guards yesterday! Have you already forgotten that we had to drag your bleeding carcasses out of the street when Gontrok and the captain cornered you?"

 Arraka's sharp laughter rings from the amulet at Flower's chest.

 "He has you there," she cackles.

 "We are sharper than we were yesterday!" Hadrik snaps. "I spent the morning drilling new techniques in the tunnels that will--"

 "A few new moves will not peel you off the stones once the captain pins you flat with his aura!" Albert shouts.

 Hadrik's teeth show through his beard in a wide grin. "Want to wager on that?"

 Albert steps forward, snarling, but the king raises a hand.

 "Enough, Albert," the king says. "We should not dismiss their claims simply because they sound absurd. Let us test them. One final trial to prove their worth. If you fail, the alliance ends, and you will be escorted from our territory."

 "If we fail, you throw us out?" Daren asks. "You would bar us from returning to cure the quarantine patients?"

 "If you still wish to risk your lives to save them after being cast out, we will permit you one final visit to administer the cure," the king says. "However, once the medicine is delivered, you leave immediately."

 "Fine," Daren says. "I accept. And if we pass, you stand with us against the palace?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Improved, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Improved)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Bag
TEXT:

