choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Improved, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Improved)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Improved
TEXT:

 "That is likely what it will come down to, yes," the king of the underground says.

 "What is the test, then?" Hadrik asks, rolling his broad shoulders. "Do you have some heavy hitters you want us to pummel?"

 The king, Albert, and Peter exchange glances before the king turns back to us.

 "In a manner of speaking," the king says. "Though tell me: have you never found it curious that someone as cautious as I am routinely conducts these audiences with almost no guard presence?"

 "It crossed my mind," Daren says. "And today you have even fewer men than before. The two royal guards outside are gone, leaving only Peter and Albert. Why dismiss your escort if you anticipated a confrontation?"

 "The answer is simple," the king says, his eyes narrowing. "They would be in the way."

 An invisible mountain of force crashes down from the vaulted ceiling. The wooden chairs beneath us splinter into kindling under the sudden weight, slamming us flat against the cold flagstones. The pressure crushes my ribs and pins my limbs to the floor; I cannot even curl my fingers against the stone. Straining my neck, I see Daren, Kate, Hadrik, and Flower crushed into the floorboards beside me. Only Melindra and Leila remain standing, untouched by the field.

 Arraka's hysterical screech rings from the gold amulet.

 "AHAHAHAHAHA!" Arraka cackles. "I wondered when you would finally show them what you were hiding! Look at them! I am dying of laughter here! AHAHAHAHAHA!"

 "What is the matter?" the king asks, unbothered by her outburst. "The trial has already begun. This is the aura manipulation technique you boasted you could overcome when you face the captain again. Show me the fruits of your training."

 The king is an aura manipulator. I never suspected he possessed magic at all. My knuckles scrape uselessly against the stone—I cannot even reach my dagger.

 "You may yield at any time," the king adds after a moment. "Admit defeat, and I release the pressure."

 "Untangle..." Daren wheezes from the floor, his face flushed dark.

 "Pardon?" the king asks.

 "Untangle... the flow of the aura... with the mind," Daren grunts, his voice rising through clenched teeth.

 He grips his hilt, forces the steel from its scabbard against the crushing tide, stabs straight up into empty air, and snaps the blade right in a savage arc. The crushing weight shatters like glass. Air floods back into my lungs, and we scramble to our feet, weapons drawn and spells flaring.

 "There it is," Arraka snickers. "See? The blockhead can manage when he actually listens."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Invisibility, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Invisibility)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Invisibility
TEXT:

 "Ah, I see," the king says, studying Daren with a nod. "You pictured untying an invisible knot with the edge of your blade. Impressive! You pass the first phase. Albert, commence the second phase when ready."

 "Yes, my king," Albert says.

 A heartbeat later, he blinks out of existence.

 "Where did he go?" Hadrik asks, scanning the empty corners with raised fists. "An invisibility cantrip?"

 "Far more than a simple spell," the king says. "Albert employs true presence concealment, mastered only by elite assassins. You cannot see him, hear his footfalls, catch his scent, or feel his aura. To your senses, he is no longer in this room."

 "Like Eiden's concealment," I say, bringing my crossbow up. "Arraka, I take it you can track him?"

 "Of course I know where he is," Arraka sneers. "But I am not spoiling a second-rate sparring match for you amateurs. If you want to pass, earn it yourselves."

 "Does not matter where he skulks," Hadrik says. "He has to reveal himself to strike, and when he does, I will grab him by the collar. Grappling does not trigger the local ward, so it should not mat-- what the?!"

 Hadrik spins and snatches at empty air behind him, catching nothing. A slit opens across the back of his leather armor, dark blood welling through the hide below his shoulder blade.

 Peter retreats toward the perimeter wall to observe.

 "He struck while concealed?" Hadrik grunts, reaching around to touch the cut. "Invisibility does not work like that!"

 "I told you, it is not merely a spell," the king says. "Disciplined breathing, total aura suppression, and his enchanted orange-hilted dagger all sustain the shroud. Years of ruthless conditioning. You underestimate assassins if you believe you can simply react to the blow. In an earnest fight, that blade would have pierced your spine, dwarf. Take this seriously."

choice("Try to listen very carefully, in order to hear Albert's footsteps.", B3-Ch05b-Hearing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Hearing, v_b3_ch5_fight = 0)
choice("Lie to Albert, telling him that I know exactly where he is.", B3-Ch05b-Bluff, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Bluff, v_b3_ch5_fight = 1)
choice("Try to concentrate and see if I can detect his aura with my magical sense.", B3-Ch05b-Observation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Observation, v_b3_ch5_fight = 2)
choice("Stand there and do nothing, hoping that everything will work out on its own.", B3-Ch05b-Nothing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Nothing, v_b3_ch5_fight = 3)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Hearing
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch5_footsteps,1) if (v_b2_ch6_stat == 1 && v_hearing >= 4)
set(v_b3_ch5_fight,0) if (v_hearing < 4 || v_b2_ch6_stat != 1)
set(v_b3_ch5_fight,4) if (v_b2_ch6_stat == 1 && v_hearing >= 4)
achievement("I can hear footsteps.",v_ac_b3_ch5_footsteps)
#if(v_hearing < 4) {
 

 No matter how skilled Albert is, his boots still touch stone. I strain to catch the scrape of leather against the floor, shutting out the vault's ambient draft. Nothing. Not a whisper of cloth or stone. I have underestimated the assassin's training.

}
#if(v_hearing >= 4 && v_b2_ch6_stat != 1) {
 

 No matter how skilled Albert is, his boots still touch stone. I strain to catch the scrape of leather against the floor, shutting out the vault's ambient draft. Even with my Hearing stat maxed, the room yields nothing. He steps without weight or sound.

 If only I had a way to force my hearing beyond its standard ceiling, I might isolate the stride, but the device offers no standard setting for that.

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_stat == 1 && v_hearing >= 4) {
 

 No matter how skilled Albert is, his boots still touch stone. I strain to catch the scrape of leather against the floor, shutting out the vault's ambient draft. Even with my Hearing stat maxed, the room yields nothing. He steps without weight or sound.

 Unless I use the secret code Melindra gave me to force a temporary stat overload.

 I yank the stat booster from my belt and slide the crossbow from my pack. Keying in Melindra's override sequence, I grip the crossbow with both hands. The surge gives me only seconds before the circuits reset.

 There. A faint, dry friction scratches against my eardrums—heel against stone, shifting weight three paces to the left. I track the stride, lead the mark, and loose a bolt into empty air, keeping clear of vital organs.

 Metal clangs hard in the dark. The bolt ricochets off an unseen dagger and clatters against the flagstones. Hadrik lunges at the spark, diving low and locking his thick arms around empty air.

 "Let go of me!" Albert's voice snarls as his form bleeds back into sight, Hadrik's grip clamped tight around his knee. "Let go, or I gut you for real!"

 "Just admit defeat and I let you up, friend!" Hadrik grins, planting his weight. "No reason to sour a friendly spar!"

 Peter raises both hands and unloads a jagged arc of lightning into Hadrik's back. The current tears through the dwarf's frame, blue arcs crackling across his skin, but Hadrik only tightens his hold. Albert does not even twitch—the electrical charge rolling off Hadrik leaves him untouched. An enchanted ward or an insulated talisman.

 "You have to hit harder than that!" Hadrik grunts through gritted teeth. "Compared to Kate's elementalist friend, that is a tickle!"

 "What is this?" Daren shouts, turning toward the throne. "Peter was not part of the agreement! You never said he would be casting--"

 Peter redirects his palms. A white-blue bolt slams directly into Daren's exposed back, cutting off his shout in a strangled gasp.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Premonition, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Premonition) if (v_hearing < 4 || v_b2_ch6_stat != 1)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Obscure, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Obscure) if (v_b2_ch6_stat == 1 && v_hearing >= 4)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Bluff
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch5_fight,0) if (v_bluff < 4)
set(v_b3_ch5_fight,5) if (v_bluff >= 4)
#if(v_bluff < 4) {
 

 "Hey, Albert, you do realize that I can tell where you are, right?" I say. "You might want to try making some less obvious movements, because right now it's like you're begging to be shot with a crossbow in the chest."

 Dead silence. Not a breath, not a scrape. The bluff falls completely flat. I need another way to draw him out.

}
#if(v_bluff >= 4) {
 

 "Hey, Albert, you do realize that I can tell where you are, right?" I say. "You might want to try making some less obvious movements, because right now it's like you're begging to be shot with a crossbow in the chest."

 The bait takes. Rapid footfalls scrape the stone as the assassin darts aside to break my supposed line of sight. Kate reacts instantly, hurling a spear of ice into the path of the sound. Steel rings against frost—the icicle shatters against an invisible blade and clatters down.

 Hadrik lunges at the impact, diving low and locking his thick arms around empty air.

 "Let go of me!" Albert's voice snarls as his form bleeds back into sight, Hadrik's grip clamped tight around his knee. "Let go, or I gut you for real!"

 "Just admit defeat and I let you up, friend!" Hadrik grins, planting his weight. "No reason to sour a friendly spar!"

 Peter raises both hands and unloads a jagged arc of lightning into Hadrik's back. The current tears through the dwarf's frame, blue arcs crackling across his skin, but Hadrik only tightens his hold. Albert does not even twitch—the electrical charge rolling off Hadrik leaves him untouched. An enchanted ward or an insulated talisman.

 "You have to hit harder than that!" Hadrik grunts through gritted teeth. "Compared to Kate's elementalist friend, that is a tickle!"

 "What is this?" Daren shouts, turning toward the throne. "Peter was not part of the agreement! You never said he would be casting--"

 Peter redirects his palms. A white-blue bolt slams directly into Daren's exposed back, cutting off his shout in a strangled gasp.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Premonition, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Premonition) if (v_bluff < 4)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Obscure, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Obscure) if (v_bluff >= 4)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Observation
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch5_fight,0) if (v_magical_sense < 4 || v_perception < 4)
set(v_b3_ch5_fight,6) if (v_magical_sense >= 4 && v_perception >= 4)
#if(v_magical_sense < 4) {
 

 The king claims Albert is concealing his aura, but focused concentration might catch a leak. I narrow my awareness, tuning out physical sight to reach for magical signatures in the cold vault air. Nothing. The only auras in the chamber belong to the people already standing in plain view.

}
#if(v_magical_sense >= 4 && v_perception < 4) {
 

 The king claims Albert is concealing his aura, but focused concentration might catch a leak. I narrow my awareness, tuning out physical sight to reach for magical signatures in the cold vault air.

 Faint, ghostly wisps of an aura drift across the chamber, but they scatter the instant I try to fix their source.

 Even with magical sense maxed, the trace is too diffuse to pinpoint his location against the flagstones.

}
#if(v_magical_sense >= 4 && v_perception >= 4) {
 

 The king claims Albert is concealing his aura, but focused concentration might catch a leak. I narrow my awareness, tuning out physical sight to reach for magical signatures in the cold vault air.

 Faint wisps of an aura drift across the room. Tracking the dispersion against the torchlight, I spot a subtle disturbance in the air—motes of floating dust parting around an invisible silhouette. It is faint enough to miss at a glance, but the displacement marks his stride clearly across the stone.

 I raise my crossbow, line up the shifting dust, and loose a bolt, aiming low to avoid vital organs.

 Metal clangs hard in the dark. The bolt ricochets off an unseen dagger with a bright yellow spark and clatters across the flagstones. Hadrik lunges at the flash, diving low and locking his thick arms around empty air.

 "Let go of me!" Albert's voice snarls as his form bleeds back into sight, Hadrik's grip clamped tight around his knee. "Let go, or I gut you for real!"

 "Just admit defeat and I let you up, friend!" Hadrik grins, planting his weight into the stone. "No reason to sour a friendly spar!"

 Peter raises both hands and unloads a jagged arc of lightning into Hadrik's back. The current tears through the dwarf's frame, blue arcs crackling across his leather armor, but Hadrik only tightens his hold. Albert does not even twitch—the electrical charge rolling off Hadrik leaves him untouched. An enchanted ward or an insulated talisman.

 "You have to hit harder than that!" Hadrik grunts through gritted teeth. "Compared to Kate's elementalist friend, that is a tickle!"

 "What is this?" Daren shouts, turning toward the throne with his blade drawn. "Peter was not part of the agreement! You never said he would be casting--"

 Peter redirects his palms. A white-blue bolt slams directly into Daren's exposed back, cutting off his shout in a strangled gasp.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Premonition, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Premonition) if (v_magical_sense < 4 || v_perception < 4)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Obscure, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Obscure) if (v_magical_sense >= 4 && v_perception >= 4)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Nothing
TEXT:

 I hold my ground, crossbow leveled, and wait. Dead silence settles over the vault, everyone tracking empty shadows and the draft of the air for the assassin's first move.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Premonition, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Premonition)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Premonition
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch5_fight,0) if (v_toughness == 0 && v_premonition < 2 || v_premonition >= 2 && v_aura_hardening < 4 || v_toughness > 0 && v_premonition < 2)
set(v_b3_ch5_fight,7) if (v_aura_hardening >= 4 && v_premonition >= 2)
#if(v_toughness == 0 && v_premonition < 2) {
 

 Hadrik runs out of patience and bellows into the empty room.

 "What is the matter, Albert? Running scared?"

 Right as the words leave Hadrik's mouth, a blade punches into the right side of my abdomen. The steel bites deep and twists before sliding free. Hot blood coats my shirt and seeps through my fingers. I press a palm hard against the puncture, gritting my teeth through the white-hot flare of pain, fighting to keep my vision from tunneling.

 "Barry!" Daren shouts, lunging toward me with hands glowing faint white. "Hold on, I am--"

 A muffled grunt cuts him off. Daren stumbles, catching himself on one knee as blood wells from behind his left knee.

 "What is wrong with you?" Daren roars at the empty air. "I was going to heal--"

 A crack of lightning cuts through his sentence. Peter's bolt hits Daren square between the shoulder blades, driving him down against the stone.

 "That is out of line!" Hadrik bellows. "Since when is Peter part of this?"

}
#if(v_toughness > 0 && v_premonition < 2) {
 

 Hadrik runs out of patience and bellows into the empty room.

 "What is the matter, Albert? Running scared?"

 Right as the words leave Hadrik's mouth, a blade punches into the right side of my abdomen. The unseen steel slices through flesh and muscle as though my toughness stat were parchment. Hot blood coats my shirt and seeps through my fingers. I press a palm hard against the puncture, gritting my teeth through the white-hot flare of pain, fighting to keep my vision from tunneling.

 "Barry!" Daren shouts, lunging toward me with hands glowing faint white. "Hold on, I am--"

 A muffled grunt cuts him off. Daren stumbles, catching himself on one knee as blood wells from behind his left knee.

 "What is wrong with you?" Daren roars at the empty air. "I was going to heal--"

 A crack of lightning cuts through his sentence. Peter's bolt hits Daren square between the shoulder blades, driving him down against the stone.

 "That is out of line!" Hadrik bellows. "Since when is Peter part of this?"

}
#if(v_premonition >= 2) {
 

 A flash of foresight hits me: a cold, unseen blade driving into my right flank, right after Hadrik taunts our opponent.

 The strike will come from the right. If I time the pivot with Hadrik's voice--

 "What is the matter, Albert?" Hadrik taunts on cue. "Running scared?"

 I spin right, driving both arms forward into the empty air. My fingers clamp onto solid wrists, tearing the shroud. Albert's outline flickers back into view, wrestling frantically against my grip.

 "Let go if you want to keep that arm," Albert warns, his voice low and vicious.

 "Taking off limbs in a mock battle?" I snap back, digging my boots into the flagstones. "Don't you think you are taking this a little too personally?"

}
#if(v_premonition >= 2 && v_aura_hardening < 4) {
 A jagged pulse of lightning rips through my chest. Agony locks my muscles in place, the electrical surge tearing my grip from Albert's wrists. He slips through my fingers, untouched by the charge, and melts back into the shadows.

 I lost him. He absorbed the lightning without flinching—an insulated charm or an electrical ward.

 "What is this?" Daren shouts, turning on the king. "Peter was not in the deal! You never said he would cast--"

 A muffled gasp cuts him off. Albert strikes from the blind spot, driving a blade into the back of Daren's knee. Blood streams down the healer's greave.

}
#if(v_aura_hardening >= 4 && v_premonition >= 2) {
 A jagged pulse of lightning rips through my chest. The current burns, but my hardened aura absorbs the worst of the impact, keeping my muscles from seizing.

 I dig my heels in and clamp down harder on Albert's wrists. The lightning leaves him completely unaffected despite my grip—an insulated charm or an electrical ward.

 "What is this?" Daren shouts, turning toward the throne. "Peter was not in the deal! You never said he would cast--"

 Peter redirects his hands. A blinding white bolt slams into Daren's exposed back, driving him down with a choked cry.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Obscure, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Obscure)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Obscure
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch5_fight < 4) {
 "Did you honestly expect phase two to consist only of Albert?" the king of the underground asks. "Peter was simply granting you a handicap. That is the only reason he held his spells."

 Kate's hand snaps out, conjuring three jagged ice cones and launching them at the throne. An invisible barrier flares, shattering the projectiles into frost powder before they reach the dais.

 "A valiant effort," the king says. "Yet insufficient."

 Once again, the crushing aura slams down from above, flattening us to the flagstones—leaving only Melindra and Leila standing. The king twists his fingers; an unseen torque wrench snaps Daren's wrist back until the sword clatters from his grip. The blade lifts into the air and drifts smoothly across the room into the king's open palm.

 "As Kate deduced, phase two was a combined assault from Albert, Peter, and myself," the king says. "We did not expect a victory, but we wanted to measure your resolve. Though you severed my initial aura lock, you failed to neutralize a single combatant in the second phase, despite having ample opportunity with Albert. Therefore, you have failed the trial."

 The crushing pressure recedes, allowing us to push up from the stone.

 "I expect your departure once you make your preparations," the king says. "Unless your vow to cure the quarantine ward remains genuine, in which case you may return one final time."

 "I do not make empty vows," Daren says, white light flaring across his own wounds before he kneels to mend the puncture in my side with soothing warmth. "I will cure every patient in that ward."

 "As you will," the king says, flicking his wrist to float Daren's sword back into the healer's grip. "Your weapon. Take better care of it. I suggest practicing basic aura manipulation. Binding your intent to a familiar object allows you to summon it from fifty paces away when disarmed. With practice, you will master the technique."

 "Thank you for the advice," Daren says, sheathing the steel with a click. "I will work on it."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_fight >= 4) {
 "Did you honestly expect phase two to consist only of Albert?" the king of the underground asks. "Peter was simply granting you a handicap. That is the only reason he held his spells."

 Kate's hand snaps out, conjuring three jagged ice cones and launching them at the throne. An invisible barrier flares, shattering the projectiles into frost powder before they reach the dais.

 "A valiant effort," the king says. "Yet insufficient."

 Once again, the crushing aura slams down from above, flattening us to the flagstones—leaving only Melindra and Leila standing. The king twists his fingers; an unseen torque wrench snaps Daren's wrist back until the sword clatters from his grip. The blade lifts into the air and drifts smoothly across the room into the king's open palm.

 "As Kate deduced, phase two was a combined assault from Albert, Peter, and myself," the king says. "We did not expect a victory, but we wanted to measure your resolve. You performed remarkably well. Pinning Albert and sustaining your grip was no small feat, and Daren severing my aura lock was unexpected. Seeing your unity in battle gives me confidence that our alliance can succeed. Congratulations. You have passed the trial."

 The crushing pressure recedes, allowing us to push up from the stone.

 "Splendid," Daren grunts, rising and dusting stone grit from his plate armor. "May I have my sword back?"

 "Of course," the king says, flicking his wrist to float Daren's sword back into the healer's grip. "Your weapon. Take better care of it. I suggest practicing basic aura manipulation. Binding your intent to a familiar object allows you to summon it from fifty paces away when disarmed. With practice, you will master the technique."

 "I have never tried manipulating aura around steel," Daren says, testing the balance of the hilt. "Worth a try. Thanks."

}
 "If you don't mind me asking," Hadrik says, looking at the throne, "how did you learn a technique as obscure as aura manipulation? And how did you hone it to this level?"

 "He learned from the captain of the royal guard," Kate says, her arms folded over her cloak. "Their methods are identical."

 "Your friend has a sharp eye," the king tells Hadrik. "The current guard captain and I served together in the royal guard decades ago. He taught me everything I know regarding aura manipulation. I could not persuade him to defect when I broke away twenty-five years ago, so we are hardly on friendly terms today. He continues to search for apprentices, but none have shown the aptitude to grasp anything beyond rudimentary tricks."

 "That works in our favor," Daren says. "If the palace fielded a legion of aura manipulators, we would stand no chance."

 "Indeed," the king says. "Now, Albert, Peter, and I must confer privately regarding the refugee crisis. If you have further questions, keep them brief."

choice(""Why did none of you attack Melindra and Leila during the test?"", B3-Ch05b-Tunnel, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Tunnel, v_b3_ch5_brief = 0)
choice(""Why did you become a royal guard?"", B3-Ch05b-Tunnel, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Tunnel, v_b3_ch5_brief = 1)
choice(""Do you and the royal guard captain have mage specializations aside from aura manipulation?"", B3-Ch05b-Tunnel, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Tunnel, v_b3_ch5_brief = 2)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Tunnel
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch5_brief == 0) {
 "Why did none of you attack Melindra and Leila during the test?"

 "Melindra brought you here precisely because the continental ward prevents her from harming locals," the king says. "Testing her would have been pointless. And as for Leila, she had no part in the Earl of Ollendor's death, so we had no grounds to expel her—especially given that she is doctor Reidzek's daughter. Whether she remains in our tunnels or marches with you against the palace is entirely her decision. You know the way to the teleporter hall. Forgive us for foregoing the escort. Farewell."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_brief == 1) {
 "Why did you become a royal guard?"

 "Youth and naivety," the king replies with a faint sigh. "The academies condition children from infancy to believe the crown exists to protect the people, and that wearing the royal crest is the highest honor in the realm. My parents kept their true thoughts secret, terrified I would repeat their grievances to an instructor. I only saw how the kingdom truly operated after I donned the armor. That realization drove me to desert the guard at twenty-nine and carve out our own settlement in these deep tunnels. I trust that satisfies your curiosity. You know the way to the teleporter hall. Forgive us for foregoing the escort. Farewell."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_brief == 2) {
 "Do you and the royal guard captain have mage specializations aside from aura manipulation?"

 "Not formal specializations, though we cast conventional spells like other mages," the king says. "The captain favors aura manipulation for its ruthless lethality, but he will deploy other disciplines if pressed into a corner. We cannot predict his full repertoire, so maintain your guard even if you seem to hold the advantage. I trust that answers your question. You know the way to the teleporter hall. Forgive us for foregoing the escort. Farewell."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_brief == 0 || v_b3_ch5_fight >= 4) {
  "Right, right," I say. "Goodbye, I guess."

 As we turn toward the archway, Peter leans down to whisper something to the king, and the king raises his voice.

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_fight < 4 && v_b3_ch5_brief != 0) {
 "Right, right," I say. "Goodbye, I guess."

 "To be clear," the king calls after us, "Leila was not subject to the trial. She had no hand in the earl's death, so we have no reason to exile her, particularly as doctor Reidzek's daughter. She remains free to join your assault against the palace if she chooses."

 "I understand," Leila writes, blue letters glowing in the air. "Thank you for letting me know."

 As we turn toward the archway, Peter leans down to whisper something to the king, and the king raises his voice.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 9) {
 "One moment..." the king says. "Peter reminds me of a development you should know before ascending. The hovering metal sentries ceased their patrols during the night. Most hang motionless above the rooftops. Breynan reports Billy Bob had cause to deactivate the Overseer after discovering his betrayal, though whether he disabled the unit or is reprogramming its parameters remains unclear. Exercise caution. Lessathi intentions are never simple."

 "Understood," Daren says. "We will stay vigilant. Farewell."

 We step through the heavy wooden door into the corridor, retracing our path toward the staging hall.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy <= 6 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 0 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8) {
 "One moment..." the king says. "Peter reminds me of a development you should know before ascending. The hovering metal sentries broke from their standard patrol routes last evening, drifting erratically across the skyline. Breynan reports you executed Billy Bob yesterday, which may explain the disruption, but stay vigilant. The Overseer is preparing something substantial."

 "Understood," Daren says. "We will stay vigilant. Farewell."

 We step through the heavy wooden door into the corridor, retracing our path toward the staging hall.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 0) {
 "One moment..." the king says. "Peter reminds me of a development you should know before ascending. The hovering metal sentries broke from their standard patrol routes last evening, drifting erratically across the skyline. Billy Bob was summoned to the palace last night; the crown may have ordered him and the Overseer to actively pursue you. Do not underestimate either of them."

 "Understood," Daren says. "We will stay vigilant. Farewell."

 We step through the heavy wooden door into the corridor, retracing our path toward the staging hall.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Workshop, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Workshop)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Workshop
TEXT:

 "Did any of you know the king and Peter were mages before they unleashed their power?" Leila's blue script hangs in the air as we walk down the tunnel. "It took me completely by surprise..."

 "You could detect magical traces in their auras if you watched closely, but the king's degree of suppression was impressive," Melindra says. "Even I did not expect him to mask that much reserve."

 "Let us hope we never face them in earnest," Daren says. "We have enough to manage with the royal guard and the garrison as it is."

 We reach the crowded waiting room a few minutes later. Daren tells us to hold position while he heads into the quarantine ward to collect Kelrim. Leila darts down the side passage toward her father's workshop to brief him before we ascend. While we wait, Flower stares into space, trying once again to reach Illuna.

 "Illuna, are you finished with that spell yet?" Flower mutters aloud, fidgeting with the strap of her top. "Illuna, please... I need to talk to you. It is about how I have been acting lately... Are you listening to me?"

 "Still shutting you out?" I ask.

 "Completely!" Flower huffs, crossing her arms. "I have no idea what she is doing. She barely even looked up while the king had us pinned flat to the floor."

 "Kelrim is with us!" Daren's voice carries across the hall as he emerges from the quarantine archway beside the goblin. "Are we set?"

 "Waiting on Leila," I say. "She went to tell her father we are heading for the--"

 A streak of silver hair rounds the corner, blue letters spelling out ahead of her: "I am back! We can move."

 "Did you sprint the entire way?" I ask as she skids to a halt beside us. "You are drenched in sweat."

 "I have the speed stat maxed, so I may as well use it," Leila writes, her chest heaving as she catches her breath. "Do not worry about me. I will recover while we walk."

choice(""Hey, I'm sure we can wait two more minutes for you to catch your breath!"", B3-Ch05b-Birthday, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Birthday, v_b3_ch5_breath = 1)
choice(""Well, if you're sure you're fine then I suppose we should get going."", B3-Ch05b-Birthday, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Birthday, v_b3_ch5_breath = 0)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Birthday
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch5_breath == 1) {
 "Hey, I'm sure we can wait two more minutes for you to catch your breath!" I say. "Right, Daren?"

 "Of course," Daren says with a warm nod. "Take a moment."

 We hold our position for a couple of minutes while Leila catches her breath, and then we turn toward the corridor leading to the teleporter chamber. Before we take three paces, Flower halts, her eyes flashing bright electric blue.

 "Wait!" Illuna's crisp voice rings out.

 "Illuna?" Flower asks. "Are you finally finished with that spell?"

 "I would not call it a masterpiece, but at least every letter is spelled correctly," Illuna says. "Step back. I need room for the plates."

 Illuna weaves her fingers through a precise sequence of gestures. A wide platter materializes on the stone before us, bearing a rich, dark layered chocolate cake surrounded by a ring of smaller porcelain plates, each set with a knife and fork. Thirty unlit wax candles bristle across the top. Frosted lettering loops across the dark surface: 'Happy birthday Flower!'.

 "Food creation is Flower's specialty, so the preparations took longer than I intended," Illuna says, "but I believe that--"

 "Today is Flower's birthday?!" Kate gasps, stepping closer to read the frosting in disbelief.

 "That is what the frosting says," Illuna replies flatly.

 "Flower, why didn't you tell us?" Kate asks, looking between them.

 "Everyone had more pressing matters to deal with, so I did not want to-- wait..."

 Flower drops to her knees beside the cake, tracing the candles one by one with an outstretched index finger.

 She finishes the count, pauses with her finger suspended, and counts them again much slower, mouthing each number in disbelief.

 "I-Illuna..." Flower whispers, her voice trembling. "Are there... are there exactly thirty candles on this cake?"

 "Of course there are," Illuna says. "Your arithmetic is intact, is it not?"

 "D-does this mean what I think it means?"

 "No, this does not mean I consider you an adult," Illuna says. "And no matter how many times you adopt a grave voice or address me as Illuna, that will not change. However... I reflected on our discussion. I arrived at the conclusion that I can make a concession and accept to call you thirty, if the number means that much to you. It will take time to accustom myself to the phrasing, but--"

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_breath == 0) {
 "Well, if you're sure you're fine then I suppose we should get going."

 We turn toward the corridor leading to the teleporter chamber. Before we take three paces, Flower halts, her eyes flashing bright electric blue.

 "Wait!" Illuna's crisp voice rings out.

 "Illuna?" Flower asks. "Are you finally finished with that spell?"

 "A few more minutes would have let me fix the spelling, but the structure holds," Illuna says. "Step back. I need room for the plates."

 Illuna weaves her fingers through a precise sequence of gestures. A wide platter materializes on the stone before us, bearing a rich, dark layered chocolate cake surrounded by a ring of smaller porcelain plates, each set with a knife and fork. Thirty unlit wax candles bristle across the top. Frosted lettering loops across the dark surface: 'Happy birday Flower!'.

 "Happy 'birday' Flower?" Hadrik asks, tilting his head with a grin.

 "It was meant to say 'birthday,' but as I noted, time ran short--" Illuna snaps, her brow furrowing.

 "Today is Flower's birthday?!" Kate gasps, stepping closer in shock.

 "That is why the cake exists," Illuna says, mildly annoyed.

 "Flower, why didn't you tell us?" Kate asks, looking between them.

 "Everyone had more pressing matters to deal with, so I did not want to-- wait..."

 Flower drops to her knees beside the cake, tracing the candles one by one with an outstretched index finger.

 She finishes the count, pauses with her finger suspended, and counts them again much slower, mouthing each number in disbelief.

 "I-Illuna..." Flower whispers, her voice trembling. "Are there... are there exactly thirty candles on this cake?"

 "Of course there are," Illuna says. "Your arithmetic is intact, is it not?"

 "D-does this mean what I think it means?"

 "No, this does not mean I consider you an adult," Illuna says. "And no matter how many times you adopt a grave voice or address me as Illuna, that will not change. However... I reflected on our discussion. I arrived at the conclusion that I can make a concession and accept to call you thirty, if the number means that much to you. It will take time to accustom myself to the phrasing, but--"

}
 "Oh Petal, thank you so much!" Flower cries, throwing both arms around her own shoulders and squeezing the shared body in a desperate, breathless hug.

 "Can you at least restrain the physical frame while you embrace me internally?" Illuna grumbles. "Do you realize how absurd we look?"

 "I don't care!" Flower shouts, squeezing harder. "I am not letting go! I don't want to fight with you anymore! Promise me we will never have another argument like that for as long as we live! I swear I won't pretend to be someone else. I won't call you Illuna anymore. Just tell me we will never go through that again!"

 "We were not fight--" Illuna begins, then sighs, the resistance draining away. "Fine. You win. I promise we will not fight again. Are you satisfied?"

 "Yes!" Flower beams, her face glowing as she releases her grip. "More than anything! Thank you!"

 She bounds over to Melindra and me, grabbing our wrists with both hands.

 "And thank you both!" Flower says, her smile radiating across the gloomy hall. "Thank you for everything you did!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Birthday2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Birthday2)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Birthday2
TEXT:

 "Hey, stop that!" Melindra yanks her hand back, pink dusting her cheeks in the first flash of embarrassment I have ever seen on her. "There is no reason to thank me! We are friends, aren't we? Besides, I barely lifted a finger!"

 "That is not true!" Flower insists. "You helped us so much! Both of you did! Petal and I would still be shouting if you hadn't stepped in! Thank you!"

 "Ahem!" Hadrik's booming cough cuts through the hall.

 While the rest of us were caught in the commotion, Hadrik fished a tinderbox from his pack and knelt by the cake. Flame dances atop all thirty candles, the yellow glow lighting the dark cavern.

 "Are you going to blow these out or let the wax drown the frosting?" Hadrik asks, lifting the platter in his thick palms.

 "Yes!" Flower laughs, darting to the cake. "Right now!"

 She draws a massive breath and blows with everything she has, snuffing every flame in a single burst of smoke and flying sparks. We chorus our birthday wishes as she takes the knife, slicing wedges of chocolate cake and handing them around.

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "It is a shame Rose isn't here with us," I say, balancing my plate. "She deserved a slice after helping with the watch last night."

 "I know..." Flower says, carving another piece. "I will conjure a fresh cake just for her the next time we meet. Here is your slice, Kelrim!"

 "Oh," Kelrim says, accepting the plate with both hands. "Thank you!"

 "Of course!" Flower smiles, glancing around our circle. "Hmm... that leaves one last slice."

 Flower conjures a final small plate and sets the last wedge upon it. She skips across the hall toward the quarantine checkpoint, holding the plate out to Desmond, who stands guard with his spear.

 "It is my birthday!" Flower announces, beaming.

 Desmond blinks down at the porcelain, utterly bewildered. "Oh... well, thanks, I suppose..."

 As she skips back to our group, Desmond calls after her into the cavern.

 "Hey! Happy birthday!"

 "Thank you!" Flower waves over her shoulder, jogging back to us.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "Here is your slice, Kelrim!" Flower says, offering the second-to-last piece to the goblin.

 "Oh," Kelrim says, accepting the plate with both hands. "Thank you!"

 "Of course!" Flower smiles, glancing around our circle. "Hmm... that leaves one last slice."

 Flower conjures a final small plate and sets the last wedge upon it. She skips across the hall toward the quarantine checkpoint, holding the plate out to Desmond, who stands guard with his spear.

 "It is my birthday!" Flower announces, beaming.

 Desmond blinks down at the porcelain, utterly bewildered. "Oh... well, thanks, I suppose..."

 As she skips back to our group, Desmond calls after her into the cavern.

 "Hey! Happy birthday!"

 "Thank you!" Flower waves over her shoulder, jogging back to us.

}
 "All the cake is served," Flower announces. "Let's move!"

 "Wait," Hadrik grunts, staring at his loaded plate. "We haven't finished eating yet."

 "You can eat while we walk!" Flower urges, tugging his leather sleeve. "Come on! No one in quarantine is dying while waiting on my account!"

 "Fair enough," Hadrik laughs. "To the teleporter."

 We carry our plates down the passage, picking our way through the tunnel. As we walk, Hadrik reaches into his pack with his free hand, producing a handful of miniature wooden mugs and setting them beside his cake. He unhitches a clay bottle of dwarven ale and pours generous splashes into each cup.

 "A proper birthday requires dwarven ale," Hadrik says, passing the cups down the line. "Just a few mouthfuls each. No getting sloppy before a skirmish! Daren, I take it the daytime abstinence holds?"

 "Without exception," Daren replies firmly.

 "Reliable as granite!" Hadrik grins, thrusting a mug into my palm.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Ending
TEXT:

 "So if I am officially thirty years old," Flower asks, her eyes wide with hope, "does that mean I get a cup of alcohol?"

 "No!" we all chorus at once.

 "Aww..." Flower pouts, her shoulders slumping.

 "Do not despair, Flower," Hadrik says, taking a hearty sip from his mug. "Life rarely hands you every prize at once, but that is no reason to waste the road. While we nurse our ale, look at the rubble beneath your feet! You could unearth a specimen that puts Melindra's collection to shame."

 "Waste of time," I say, glancing at the debris along the wall. "The stones in this stratum are useless for a collection. Half of them are fractured chips, and the rest are either the wrong shade of sedimentary gray or worn too smooth by groundwater seepage. Melindra already picked the prime mineral specimens when we passed through yesterday."

 Dead silence drops over the corridor. Everyone stops mid-stride to stare at me in sheer disbelief. Flower gasps loudly, clamping both hands over her mouth.

 "Well, look at that," Melindra purrs, a triumphant smirk spreading across her face as she claps me hard on the back. "A whole night shift of mineralogy lectures was not wasted after all! We will make a proper collector out of you yet, Barry!"

 "Do not hold your breath," I tell her.

 "Oh my gods!" Flower squeals, practically vibrating with excitement. "Barry actually knows which rocks we like! I can't believe it! This is the best birthday ever!"

 We spend the rest of the trek to the teleporter chamber savoring chocolate cake, passing dwarven ale, and helping Flower gather distinctive stones from the bedrock to launch her new collection. The celebration was brief, carved out of a crisis, but her joy is infectious. By the time the last crumbs are gone, our fatigue has lifted, and our resolve is set for the road above.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch05b-Credits, v_current_scene = B3-Ch05b-Credits)


ID: B3-Ch05b-Credits
TEXT:

 The 6th chapter of Book 3 is now available. Tap on the button at the bottom of this page to proceed.

----------------------------------------

Sorry for the long wait. The delay was caused by some health problems that I had back in November and December and by the extremely complicated path variation in this chapter. (the chapter has 10 achievements in total)

Hint: Some of the achievements in this chapter are on paths that can differ depending on how you interacted with the Overseer at the end of chapter 4 of book 3. There will also be other factors influencing the variation of the paths, like whether or not Rose is alive and whether you passed the king of the underground's test in the previous chapter or not.

Also, remember that every chapter in Magium can be finished without having any points invested in your stats, so if you find yourself unable to pass a stat check, try loading your last checkpoint and redoing some of your previous choices until you find the paths that require no stats.

Good luck!

